


🧩 IPC (Inter-Process Communication)
IPC allows different processes to exchange data or signals.

🔹 1. Unnamed Pipe
🧠 Concept:
Simple one-way communication between related processes (usually parent ↔ child).

Exists only in memory while the processes are running.

⚙️ How it works:
Created using pipe() system call.

Data written at one end (write-end) is read from the other end (read-end).

int fd[2];
pipe(fd);
write(fd[1], "hello", 5);
read(fd[0], buf, 5);
✅ Use when:
You just need simple communication between parent and child processes.

Example: Shell pipeline — ls | grep ".cpp"

❌ Don’t use when:
You need unrelated processes to communicate (use Named Pipe instead).

You need bidirectional communication (each pipe is one-way).

🔹 2. Named Pipe (FIFO)
🧠 Concept:
Like an unnamed pipe, but has a name (file entry) in the filesystem.

Allows communication between unrelated processes.

⚙️ How it works:
Created with mkfifo("myfifo", 0666);

Processes open it using open() and read/write.

mkfifo /tmp/myfifo
# Terminal 1
cat /tmp/myfifo
# Terminal 2
echo "Hello" > /tmp/myfifo
✅ Use when:
Processes are independent and need stream-like communication.

You want to connect programs through a known file path.

❌ Don’t use when:
You need high throughput or random access — it’s sequential only.

🔹 3. Shared Memory
🧠 Concept:
Fastest IPC — processes share a region of physical memory.

No data copying; both can directly read/write the same area.

⚙️ How it works:
Use shmget() and shmat() (System V) or mmap() (POSIX).

Needs synchronization (e.g., semaphores, mutex) to avoid race conditions.

int shmid = shmget(key, 1024, IPC_CREAT|0666);
char *data = (char*) shmat(shmid, NULL, 0);
✅ Use when:
You need very fast data exchange (like large data, video frames, sensor data).

Processes are on the same machine.

❌ Don’t use when:
You need communication across machines.

You can’t easily manage synchronization yourself.

🔹 4. Message Queue
🧠 Concept:
Kernel-managed queue where messages are sent/received in structured format (message + type).

Supports asynchronous communication.

⚙️ How it works:
Use msgget(), msgsnd(), msgrcv().

msgsnd(msqid, &msg, sizeof(msg), 0);
msgrcv(msqid, &msg, sizeof(msg), 0, 0);
✅ Use when:
You need to send discrete messages, not continuous streams.

You need to prioritize or buffer messages.

Producer-consumer style communication.

❌ Don’t use when:
You need very high speed (shared memory is faster).

Message size is large (limited by kernel queue size).

🔹 5. Socket
🧠 Concept:
Most flexible IPC method — can work locally (Unix domain socket) or remotely (TCP/UDP).

Uses network protocols for bidirectional communication.

⚙️ How it works:
Use socket(), bind(), listen(), accept() etc.

Two processes communicate like client and server.

int sock = socket(AF_UNIX, SOCK_STREAM, 0);
✅ Use when:
You need network or cross-machine communication.

You’re building client-server, distributed, or microservice architectures.

❌ Don’t use when:
Both processes are local and performance-critical (shared memory is faster).

🔹 6. Memory-Mapped File (mmap)
🧠 Concept:
Maps a file into memory so processes can read/write the file directly through memory.

Persistent (changes reflect in the file).

⚙️ How it works:
Use mmap() system call on an open file descriptor.

The same file can be mapped by multiple processes.

int fd = open("data.bin", O_RDWR);
void* ptr = mmap(NULL, size, PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE, MAP_SHARED, fd, 0);
✅ Use when:
You want shared memory + file persistence.

You need to share large data between processes and save it to disk.

Example: Database caches, file-backed shared states.

❌ Don’t use when:
You don’t need persistence or file I/O overhead